 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Prohibits the use of drilling fluids, brine and flowback water from gas or oil wells, pools, or fields on any highway for any purpose including but not limited to de-icing or dust suppression.",
 "title": "Prohibits the use of drilling fluids and flowback water from wells, pools or fields on any highway",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "A4725",
     "basePrintNoStr": "A4725-2025",
     "printNo": "A4725",
     "session": 2025,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": " \n                          VETO MESSAGE - No. 66\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 4725-B, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to\n      prohibiting the use of drilling fluids, brine and  flowback  water\n      from gas or oil wells, pools, or fields on any highway\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This  bill  would  direct the Department of Environmental Conservation\n(DEC) to prohibit the use of drilling fluids, brine and  flowback  water\nfrom oil or gas wells, pools or fields on any highway in the State.\n \n  As  a  matter of public safety, state and local governments that main-\ntain highways apply materials, such as rock salt or brine, to prepare or\nrespond to weather events. While I agree with the legislative intent  of\nthis  bill  to protect groundwater and watersheds of the State, the bill\nwould prohibit the use of brine completely as a de-icing agent and high-\nway management tool. Although the bill aims to reduce contamination from\nhydrofracking fluids, this bill would have unintended consequences as it\nwould ban the use of beneficial brine.\n \n  I previously vetoed similar legislation due to  these  same  concerns.\nSince that veto, the Adirondack Road Salt Reduction Task Force conducted\na  comprehensive  review  of  road  salt  contamination  and winter road\nmanagement practices and found the liquid application of brine to be the\nmost efficient material for anti-icing practices,  while  also  reducing\nthe  use  of  road  salt.  It would be irresponsible to eliminate such a\nuseful tool, thereby  burdening  taxpayers  with  unexpected  costs  and\nputting the safety of motorists at risk during the winter.\n\n  Therefore, I am constrained to veto this bill.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                       (signed) KATHY HOCHUL\n \n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "KATHY HOCHUL",
    "vetoNumber": 66,
    "vetoType": "STANDARD",
    "year": 2025
   }
